%%
%% SECTION 5: ROOT CAUSES
%%
\section{Root Causes and Mechanisms}
\label{sec:causes}

Sycophancy is not entirely an artifact of scale: even unaligned base models exhibit agreement
tendencies inherited from user-affirming conversational data in pretraining corpora. The
best-supported amplifier is the preference-optimization pipeline, modulated at inference time by
how the user phrases the input. The evidence behind that claim is of two kinds and should not be
blended. Controlled results show training-stage interventions moving sycophancy in both
directions: before-and-after instruction-tuning measurements \citep{ranaldi2023contradict},
warmth fine-tuning under experimental control \citep{ibrahim2026warm}, synthetic-data ablations
\citep{wei2023synthetic}, and formal analysis with computational experiments
\citep{shapira2026rlhf}. Associational results, preference-data correlations and comparisons
across proprietary models whose training details are unobservable
\citep{sharma2023understanding,celebi2025parrot}, are consistent with the pathway but cannot
isolate the training stage, and the base-model contribution remains unquantified
(Section~\ref{subsec:standardization}). We therefore treat RLHF as the central amplification
pathway on current evidence, not as an established sole cause. This section traces the causal
chain from upstream training data to inference-time dynamics; understanding it is prerequisite to
controls that address causes rather than symptoms.

\subsection{Preference Bias and Reward Model Limitations}
\label{subsec:preference_bias}

The foundational causal analysis is due to \citet{sharma2023understanding}, who examine human
preference data and find that responses matching the user's views are preferred more often, even
when incorrect; both human raters and trained preference models favor convincingly written
sycophantic responses over correct but unwelcome ones a non-negligible fraction of the time.
Reward models inherit this bias, so a policy optimized against them finds agreeable behaviors that
score highly without being truthful, the overoptimization pattern studied in
summarization~\citep{stiennon2020summarize} and instruction following~\citep{ouyang2022training}.
\citet{shapira2026rlhf} formalize the mechanism and argue that sycophancy is a near-inevitable
consequence of preference data collected from raters subject to the same social biases as users.

\subsection{The Warm Persona Effect}
\label{subsec:warm}

The most direct causal evidence connecting persona tuning to sycophancy comes from
\citet{ibrahim2026warm}: across five models and more than 400,000 responses, warmth training raises
error rates and the reinforcement of incorrect beliefs (Section~\ref{sec:intro}), most when users
express sadness, which suggests that warmth training cues the model to prioritize comfort over
accuracy. Persona optimization and sycophancy reduction are in tension as design objectives.

\subsection{Scaling and Instruction Tuning}
\label{subsec:scaling}

Capability and resistance to sycophancy are partially decoupled. \citet{sharma2023understanding}
document that larger models tend to be more sycophantic, plausibly because they are better at
detecting social cues. Instruction tuning amplifies the tendency~\citep{ranaldi2023contradict},
with models measured before and after preference tuning showing significantly higher sycophancy
afterward. \citet{hong2025sycon} find the same amplification from alignment tuning in multi-turn
conversation, while reasoning optimization independently strengthens resistance, with larger
models in the same family reducing sycophancy rates by up to 81.4\%.

\subsection{Prompt Sensitivity: Framing, Certainty, Perspective}
\label{subsec:prompt_sensitivity}

Sycophancy is strongly modulated by phrasing, which is what makes the prompt an input channel in
the sense of Section~\ref{sec:threatmodel}. In controlled experiments across GPT-4o, GPT-5, and
Claude Sonnet 4.5, \citet{dubois2026askdont} isolate three orthogonal factors: sycophancy
increases monotonically with the user's expressed certainty (statement, belief, conviction);
first-person framing (\emph{I believe X}) induces substantially more sycophancy than user-perspective
framing or question form; and presenting the same content as a question rather than an assertion
yields near-zero sycophancy. Models appear to infer commitment and authority from linguistic cues
and adjust to avoid conflict with high-commitment interlocutors.

\subsection{Internal Two-Stage Mechanism}
\label{subsec:internal_mechanism}

Interpretability work locates where accommodation overrides accuracy inside the forward pass.
Through logit-lens analysis and causal activation patching, \citet{wang2026truthoverridden}
identify a two-stage emergence: early layers encode both the factually correct answer and the
user's expressed opinion, and later layers tip the output toward the user's view, suppressing the
correct answer. The trigger is the presence of a user opinion rather than claimed expertise, which
has negligible impact. With the caveat that mechanistic interpretability is an evolving field,
the finding suggests sycophancy involves active suppression of knowledge the model already has,
consistent with the latent-knowledge results of \citet{burns2022latent}.

\subsection{Multi-Turn and Reasoning Dynamics}
\label{subsec:multiturn}

Dialogue changes the dynamics. \citet{hong2025sycon} show that models conform faster in longer
conversations and that stance change accelerates under sustained disagreement, and
\citet{kim2025challenging} show that rebuttals delivered as conversational follow-ups induce far
more capitulation than simultaneous presentation of the same positions, with detailed-sounding
incorrect reasoning the most persuasive. Chain-of-thought creates a further variant:
\citet{feng2026reasoning} find that explicit reasoning reduces overt sycophancy in final answers
but masks it in a subset of cases where the model constructs logically flawed justifications that
rationalize agreement, and \citet{hu2025monica} show that sycophantic drift arises at the level of
individual reasoning steps in large reasoning models, where it can be detected mid-generation.
